#{[{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198088 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/id #uuid "ed6483ae-971a-543b-adb6-72ac5809b602" :question/math-academy-id "e-12662" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})= -x_1^2+2x_2^2+6x_3^2+4x_1x_2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $6.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the maximum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/e2/e207d971031273136f2c8ddc4e4f4ea462a4f7d8aa9c893b0973101a0e2e24f4.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}=6{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-6I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 6\\end{bmatrix}-6\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-7 & 2 & 0 \\\\ 2 & -4 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-6I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/ea/eab2969eff48fccb1fc2c4e91181e83b71707f80d1e315e07293aeb5b9011704.svg)   \n\nSince $\\mathbf{v}$ is already normalized ($\\|\\mathbf{v}\\|=1$), we conclude that,   ![](images/98/98be1156a6de649bcd1aee6f38b2c71447089e477b50150795517d5704e3fa25.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198089 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/id #uuid "8a1adb2a-e9c5-52e1-bfa0-6977884fae42" :question/math-academy-id "e-12663" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(\\mathbf{x})=9x_1^2-24x_1x_2+16x_2^2,$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1,$ is equal to $0.$ Find a vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is attained." :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\max \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x}),$ when $\\| \\mathbf{x} \\|=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A{:}$\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q(\\mathbf{x}) \\: | \\:  \\| \\mathbf{x} \\| = 1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector $\\mathbf{u}_{\\textrm{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n![](images/da/da2ae479bd2bb629add35ffd9f0a66bc96256ad380b7f61789893d9bc9be6ef0.svg)\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=0{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-0I & =\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}9 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 16\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-0I)\\mathbf{x}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/95/955f504032fc42c502d4cd1bb8f68645930cdf2b4a08a48e80eea2ac6214acc9.svg)  \n\nFinally, dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = 5,$ we get \n\n![](images/2f/2f70f885f2773dc48443efba4c673d6d19f443b4fc1ad8f135e2cab95fad21ea.svg)"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198090 :knowledge-point/_canonical-example [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/id #uuid "a6abb2f6-6e59-59ac-8549-62d25b3b6e9b" :question/math-academy-id "e-12664" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(\\mathbf{x})=16x_1^2+16x_1x_2+x_2^2$ given that $16x_1^2 + x_2^2=16,$ and find the vector $\\mathbf{x}$ at which the minimum is obtained." :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n![](images/ef/ef4c1994a6c3980934ab8b95a92be32293c434fa84a0ffb70dc82cf3198367b2.svg)\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\dfrac{x_1^2}{1^2}+\\dfrac{x_2^2}{4^2}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=1$ (along the $x_1$-axis) and $b=4$ (along the $x_2$-axis). \n\nIf we introduce new variables\n\n$\\qquad$ $y_1={x_1} \\quad$ and $\\quad y_2 = \\dfrac{x_2}{4},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into $y_1^2+y_2^2 = 1,$ or $\\|\\mathbf{y}\\|=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n![](images/df/dfa724469e02e9851ec5dc895fe7dda810836745d3a1781a8c60f01d09fbd32b.svg) \n\nwe obtain that $\\mathbf{x} = P \\mathbf{y}.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(\\mathbf{x}) = \\mathbf{x}^T \\! A \\mathbf{x} = (P\\mathbf{y})^T A \\, (P\\mathbf{y}) = \\mathbf{y}^T \\! (P^TAP) \\mathbf{y}= Q'(\\mathbf{y}).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since $P^T=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 \\\\ 8 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 8 \\\\ 32 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we minimise $Q'(\\mathbf{y})$ when $\\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ \\begin{vmatrix}16-\\lambda  & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16-\\lambda \\end{vmatrix} & =0 \\\\ (16-\\lambda )(16-\\lambda )-1\\,024 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}-32\\lambda -768 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -48)(\\lambda +16) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =48,\\,-16\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\n\\min \\{ Q'(\\mathbf{y}) \\: | \\: \\| \\mathbf{y} \\|=1 \\} = \\lambda_{\\textrm{min}} = -16.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue $\\lambda_{\\textrm{min}}=-16{:}$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-(-16I) & =\\begin{bmatrix}16 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 16\\end{bmatrix}+16\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}32 & 32 \\\\ 32 & 32\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-(-16I))\\mathbf{y}=\\mathbf{0}$ gives the eigenvector ![](images/49/49d16ecc1b8c785d665bd961f24f5627f18fceec2a74dee5b2b1f4c39f2864a8.svg) Dividing $\\mathbf{v}$ by its norm $\\| \\mathbf{v} \\| = \\sqrt{2},$ we get \n![](images/18/18d7c92aca6dc096a79cf0ae8ea6ef99e058da58481689af353f430e726b69a5.svg) \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{min}} & =P{u}_{\\text{min}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198560 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "5991713d-c756-5033-8e5a-4d4d4ed69367" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410175} {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} {:answer/id #uuid "7a09264a-e062-5b32-b960-ce1fdd0596f3" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410180} {:answer/id #uuid "a18e2980-f7b3-5399-bdf5-0e6bab2f2d73" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 0 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410183} {:answer/id #uuid "f781d714-082f-5b5c-9033-4f6f86bf4d82" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410188}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5dd4da5f-a084-569f-9c7d-65ba6f2fa2d4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410176} :answer-field/id #uuid "9a69a67c-d671-56dc-a201-31f05adf2afb" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186410193}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "13f589cb-239b-530f-99ce-e58d907d8d98" :question/math-academy-id "q-136828" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-{x}_{1}^{2}-{x}_{2}^{2}+2{x}_{3}^{2}+2{x}_{1}{x}_{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $-2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-(-2)I & =\\begin{bmatrix}-1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & -1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}-(-2)\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 1 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-(-2)I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198561 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "fb041804-f145-50b4-816c-fcab9c17bcdd" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388519} {:answer/id #uuid "b2e13d89-d82e-50de-b738-ce617b4eb4c6" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388520} {:answer/id #uuid "4da080a5-de45-50af-a433-06854c96c579" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388521} {:answer/id #uuid "3e2330d3-d405-54f2-a9a6-48d3b6275ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} {:answer/id #uuid "3e679990-9d14-53c0-a924-3aa2912773d8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388523}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "3e2330d3-d405-54f2-a9a6-48d3b6275ab4" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388522} :answer-field/id #uuid "c1c95db7-7a90-557c-8b94-0b154d58f86d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388518}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "3e8e756a-b2ab-5c3c-9c14-7a7f8b61663e" :question/math-academy-id "q-138085" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $2.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=2:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-2I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-2\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -1 \\\\ -1 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-2I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$  \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186388524 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198442 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "a9d4e49c-202b-564f-839d-64b5d71a3783" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Are Attained on the Unit Sphere"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "659e6953-7cee-5b96-970f-fa397b86ee27" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388526} {:answer/id #uuid "326d7edd-18b0-5a3b-b08d-0b77a365bbd8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{3}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388527} {:answer/id #uuid "b7f8657c-b325-5796-aed4-2807063c509c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 0 \\\\ -1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388528} {:answer/id #uuid "aeba8346-f317-5cca-9056-2ca0f6aec458" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388529} {:answer/id #uuid "76dc6aea-81a8-5e26-8056-6496647263c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388530}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "76dc6aea-81a8-5e26-8056-6496647263c1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388530} :answer-field/id #uuid "43aa64d2-1826-583b-afa1-0a5a086f07b0" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186388525}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "aacc9ef4-d33c-5e64-bc37-708eaf7597fc" :question/math-academy-id "q-138090" :question/problem "The minimum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=3{x}_{1}^{2}+{x}_{2}^{2}+3{x}_{3}^{2}-2{x}_{1}{x}_{3},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $1.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the minimum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=1:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-I & =\\begin{bmatrix}3 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}-\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 & -1 \\\\ 0 & 0 & 0 \\\\ -1 & 0 & 2\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nSo, we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{min}}=\\begin{bmatrix}0 \\\\ 1 \\\\ 0\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542227 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "4b084a71-1f9e-54dd-851a-82a512a9441f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541576} {:answer/id #uuid "51fb79b7-312a-53eb-918f-6f6757c1cf62" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{2\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}2 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541589} {:answer/id #uuid "7476878f-31ec-5872-a987-7e9e201cce2d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ -1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541661} {:answer/id #uuid "a4c7c0f8-394d-59c4-a369-40c36e6330fe" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}0 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541762} {:answer/id #uuid "d4a1a9b5-c93a-5c68-be93-e1db0cd98414" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541875}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "4b084a71-1f9e-54dd-851a-82a512a9441f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541576} :answer-field/id #uuid "f7b7ca88-f1ff-5236-b465-788cafd4842d" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186542100}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "f0a5dfca-c787-55e8-9cb2-9d81fe185f23" :question/math-academy-id "q-138129" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)=-3{x}_{1}^{2}+8{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+3{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $5.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin\\{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=5:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-5I & =[\\begin{aligned}-3 & 4 \\\\ 4 & 3\\end{aligned}]-5[\\begin{aligned}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{aligned}]=[\\begin{aligned}-8 & 4 \\\\ 4 & -2\\end{aligned}]\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-5I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{5},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 2\\end{aligned}].\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198684 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "084f7ce5-201e-5ad6-9bce-d9e103302f97" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281759} {:answer/id #uuid "1a0ebcf9-e9a6-5cca-97f6-85bfa563e4de" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 6\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186281926} {:answer/id #uuid "b4cebb42-cb22-5ab6-8b45-cbd08c48d13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} {:answer/id #uuid "c2dcb565-f7cc-56d6-babf-2810305e6f8c" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283387} {:answer/id #uuid "dd543f2a-9082-5f1c-b191-79aeac5df263" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 2\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283619}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "b4cebb42-cb22-5ab6-8b45-cbd08c48d13e" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283255} :answer-field/id #uuid "bd7dce51-5a8d-500a-a178-44f5571990b6" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284272}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045486 :db/ident :question.difficulty/hard} :question/id #uuid "38171cb4-52a5-5ebe-b692-159c5b30da34" :question/math-academy-id "q-170138" :question/problem "The maximum value of $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-4{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $9{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=36,$ is $40.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{3}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=3$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{3},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinate matrix \n\n$$\nP=\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix},\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -2 \\\\ -2 & 4\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & -4 \\\\ -6 & 12\\end{bmatrix}\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{bmatrix}.\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual.\n\nSince we are told that the maximum value of the quadratic form is $40,$ we have\n\n$$\nmax\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{max}=40.\n$$\n\nNext, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=40:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B-40I & =\\begin{bmatrix}4 & -12 \\\\ -12 & 36\\end{bmatrix}-40\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-36 & -12 \\\\ -12 & -4\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(B-40I)y=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.$ Dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{10},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$\n\n \n\nFinally, we map this vector back to the initial coordinate  system using the change-of-coordinates matrix:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}{x}_{\\text{max}} & =P{u}_{\\text{max}} \\\\  & =\\begin{bmatrix}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 3\\end{bmatrix}\\cdot \\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 3\\end{bmatrix} \\\\  & =\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 9\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198685 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198444 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "17fd5c3d-d4c6-5f78-b262-fbc6b7209501" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained Over an Ellipse"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "964ff685-0c8f-5399-a6db-e7e9ff284b45" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-80" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194742} {:answer/id #uuid "c4372638-eac2-5651-a42e-b474aa8e5014" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-160" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194743} {:answer/id #uuid "e585951e-1933-5d5a-a55e-f897fdd4664a" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-100" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194744} {:answer/id #uuid "135fadcd-1711-57c3-92f2-65d7240f6b44" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-40" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194745} {:answer/id #uuid "5642d232-0433-5476-bf0e-5697fbc0a389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "5642d232-0433-5476-bf0e-5697fbc0a389" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "-140" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194746} :answer-field/id #uuid "223a3270-62e6-5297-b31c-f1bd2215a506" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186194741}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045487 :db/ident :question.difficulty/moderate} :question/id #uuid "8a89d201-6c7f-5b6a-9c34-e445b93c0f7e" :question/math-academy-id "q-170160" :question/problem "Find the minimum value of $Q(x)=5{x}_{1}^{2}-16{x}_{1}{x}_{2}-4{x}_{2}^{2}$ given that $25{x}_{1}^{2}+4{x}_{2}^{2}=100.$" :question/worked-solution "First, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=[\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{aligned}]\n$$\n\nNotice that we can write the constraint equation as follows:\n\n$$\n\\frac{{x}_{1}^{2}}{{2}^{2}}+\\frac{{x}_{2}^{2}}{{5}^{2}}=1\n$$\n\nThis is an ellipse with semiaxes $a=2$ (along the ${x}_{1}$-axis) and $b=5$ (along the ${x}_{2}$-axis). If we introduce new variables\n\n$\\,$ ${y}_{1}=\\frac{{x}_{1}}{2}\\,$ and $\\,{y}_{2}=\\frac{{x}_{2}}{5},$\n\nthe equation of the ellipse will transform into ${y}_{1}^{2}+{y}_{2}^{2}=1$ or $‖y‖=1.$\n\nSo, introducing a change-of-coordinates matrix \n\n$$\nP=[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}],\n$$\n\n \n\nwe obtain that $x=Py.$ Substituting this into the expression of the quadratic form, we get\n\n$$\nQ(x)={x}^{T}\\,Ax=(Py)^{T}A\\,(Py)={y}^{T}\\,({P}^{T}AP)y={Q}^{′}(y).\n$$\n\nAs a result, since ${P}^{T}=P,$ the matrix of our quadratic form in new coordinates is \n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}B & ={P}^{T}\\,AP \\\\  & =PAP \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}5 & -8 \\\\ -8 & -4\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}10 & -16 \\\\ -40 & -20\\end{aligned}][\\begin{aligned}2 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 5\\end{aligned}] \\\\  & =[\\begin{aligned}20 & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100\\end{aligned}].\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\n \n\nNow, we maximize ${Q}^{′}(y)$ when $‖y‖=1,$ as usual. Calculating the eigenvalues $B,$ we obtain the following:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}|B-\\lambda I| & =0 \\\\ |\\begin{aligned}20-\\lambda  & -80 \\\\ -80 & -100-\\lambda \\end{aligned}| & =0 \\\\ (20-\\lambda )(-100-\\lambda )-6\\,400 & =0 \\\\ {\\lambda }^{2}+80\\lambda -8\\,400 & =0 \\\\ (\\lambda -60)(\\lambda +140) & =0 \\\\ \\lambda  & =60,\\,-140\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nTherefore, we have\n\n$$\nmin\\{{Q}^{′}(y)\\,|\\,‖y‖=1\\}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}=-140.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186284630 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "390ae2c8-dec1-5d3f-9c6b-1792d16e78b7" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282190} {:answer/id #uuid "4e373f20-5a94-571c-b052-8a90de7fdc5f" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{5}}\\begin{bmatrix}-2 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282363} {:answer/id #uuid "62273b79-193d-54d1-862d-e78f13b5f7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282526} {:answer/id #uuid "9376d9e5-6005-5501-a544-4fcc88cf2498" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{10}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ -3\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282959} {:answer/id #uuid "9ac240dd-95e7-55ec-a84e-d3b0436abcff" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186283039}] :answer-field/correct {:answer/id #uuid "62273b79-193d-54d1-862d-e78f13b5f7f8" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186282526} :answer-field/id #uuid "7f8ccfc5-1cac-5ad6-b112-c28a3ec8eaf1" :answer-field/key "selection" :answer-field/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045493 :db/ident :answer-field.type/radio} :db/id #edb/ref 17592186284148}] :question/difficulty {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045485 :db/ident :question.difficulty/easy} :question/id #uuid "1db2761c-31e6-54a0-9828-fe3bf1a8a7c5" :question/math-academy-id "q-170377" :question/problem "The maximum value of the quadratic form $Q(x)={x}_{1}^{2}-6{x}_{1}{x}_{2}+{x}_{2}^{2},$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to $4.$ Which of the following is a vector $x$ at which the maximum is attained?" :question/worked-solution "Let $A$ be the matrix corresponding to our quadratic form. Recall that: \n\n- The maximum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the largest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmax{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}\n$$\n\n \nThe maximum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{max}}.$\n\n- The minimum value of $Q(x),$ when $‖x‖=1,$ is equal to the smallest eigenvalue of $A:$\n\n$$\nmin{Q(x)\\,|\\,‖x‖=1}={\\lambda }_{\\text{min}}\n$$\n\n \nThe minimum is attained on the corresponding unit eigenvector ${u}_{\\text{min}}.$\n\nFirst, we write down the matrix of our quadratic form:\n\n$$\nA=\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}\n$$\n\nNow, we need the unit eigenvector that corresponds to the eigenvalue ${\\lambda }_{\\text{max}}=4:$\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}A-4I & =\\begin{bmatrix}1 & -3 \\\\ -3 & 1\\end{bmatrix}-4\\begin{bmatrix}1 & 0 \\\\ 0 & 1\\end{bmatrix}=\\begin{bmatrix}-3 & -3 \\\\ -3 & -3\\end{bmatrix}\\end{aligned}\n$$\n\nSeeking a non-zero solution of $(A-4I)x=0$ gives the eigenvector $v=\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.$ \n\nFinally, dividing $v$ by its norm $‖v‖=\\sqrt{2},$ we get \n\n$$\n{u}_{\\text{max}}=\\frac{v}{‖v‖}=\\frac{1}{\\sqrt{2}}\\begin{bmatrix}-1 \\\\ 1\\end{bmatrix}.\n$$"}] [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186542191 :knowledge-point/_questions [{:db/id #edb/ref 17592186198443 :knowledge-point/id #uuid "6115493b-482c-5360-8d19-52582228e21d" :knowledge-point/title "Finding Where the Extrema of a Quadratic Form Is Attained on the Unit Circle"}] :question/answer-fields [{:answer-field/choices [{:answer/id #uuid "63493919-14e9-5870-9f2f-d3d0b3c6ca3d" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "\\frac{1}{10}[\\begin{aligned}-3 \\\\ 1\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541629} {:answer/id #uuid "6b42332f-6a16-50fb-a17d-46adbcaf4ace" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 17592186045498 :db/ident :answer.type/math} :answer/value "[\\begin{aligned}1 \\\\ 3\\end{aligned}]" :db/id #edb/ref 17592186541638} {:answer/id #uuid "91418d79-29a9-572d-b4d7-bafef27445b1" :answer/type {:db/id #edb/ref 